---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import ForFramework from '@/components/ForFramework.astro';
import Code from '@/components/Code.astro';
import Choices from '@/components/motion/Choices.astro';
import Choice from '@/components/motion/Choice.astro';
import GoodToKnow from '@/components/motion/GoodToKnow.astro';
import Fact from '@/components/motion/Fact.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import { MotionScrollReact } from '@/examples/react/motion-scroll.tsx';
import { MotionScrollAngular } from '@/examples/angular/motion-scroll.ts';
import MotionScrollSvelte from '@/examples/svelte/motion-scroll.svelte';
import { glossaryHref } from '@/lib/motion-glossary';

// Without a container, the nearest scrollable ancestor of the target scrolls, or the page.
const scrollToSection = `import { scrollTo } from '@udixio/motion/scroll';

// Scroll the page to the pricing section, and give the section the focus once it is there.
const { finished } = scrollTo(document.getElementById('pricing')!, { focus: true });

// 'finished' once the section is in place, or 'cancelled' when the visitor scrolled first.
const result = await finished;`;
---
<MotionPage
  slug="scroll"
  title="Motion: scroll to a target"
  description="Scroll to an element or a position over a spring, in HTML, React, Angular and Svelte. A layout that moves while the scroll runs is followed, and the visitor's own scrolling stops it."
  frameworkSwitch
>
  <Fragment slot="summary">
    Scrolls a box or the page to a target over a spring, and the visitor's own scrolling stops it.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="when-to-use-it">When to use it</h2>
  <Choices size="compact">
    <Choice lead title="scrollTo" href="/motion/choosing-an-api#scroll" link="Compare in Choosing an API">
      <Fragment slot="when">a button or a handler brings the visitor to a target: a section, a line of lyrics, the current track.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="because">the destination is read on each frame, so a layout that moves during the scroll is followed, and the focus can go to the target on arrival.</Fragment>
    </Choice>
    <Choice code={false} title="Reveal" when="Prefer it when" href="/motion/reveal" link="Reveal">
      <Fragment slot="when">the element should play as it enters the view, with no button and no call.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="because">the view decides when it plays: the visitor only scrolls.</Fragment>
    </Choice>
    <Choice code={false} title="Scrub" when="Prefer it when" href="/motion/scrub" link="Scrub">
      <Fragment slot="when">the motion should follow the scroll itself, frame by frame, such as a progress bar.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="because">the scroll position sets the progress, and the motion runs back when the visitor scrolls back.</Fragment>
    </Choice>
  </Choices>

  <h2 id="scroll-the-queue-to-the-current-track">Scroll the queue to the current track</h2>
  <p>
    The queue is a box with its own scroll. The button brings the current track to the top of the box, and gives that
    track the focus.
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">Find the queue and the track with <code>getElementById</code>, and call <code>scrollTo()</code> from the button's click listener.</p>
    <p slot="react">Keep the queue and the track in <code>useRef</code>, and call <code>scrollTo()</code> from the button's <code>onClick</code>.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Read the queue and the rows with <code>viewChild</code> and <code>viewChildren</code>, and call <code>scrollTo()</code> from a method that the button's <code>(click)</code> runs.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Bind the queue and the rows with <code>bind:this</code>, and call <code>scrollTo()</code> from the button's <code>onclick</code>.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>Set <code>container</code> to the queue: it is the element that scrolls.</p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-scroll" layout="split" previewHeight={320}>
    <MotionScrollReact slot="react" client:visible />
    <MotionScrollAngular slot="angular" client:visible />
    <MotionScrollSvelte slot="svelte" client:visible />
  </MotionUsage>

  <h2 id="how-it-behaves">How it behaves</h2>
  <h3 id="what-starts-it">What starts it</h3>
  <p>
    A call to <code>scrollTo()</code> starts it, from a click handler or from any function. Nothing has to be bound to the
    element first, so the call reads the same in every framework.
  </p>
  <p>
    The scroll moves on the slow <a href={glossaryHref('spatial and effects springs')}>spatial</a> spring, and reads its
    destination on every frame. Its <code>finished</code> promise resolves to <code>'finished'</code> at the destination,
    or to <code>'cancelled'</code> when the scroll stops first.
  </p>

  <h3 id="rules">Rules</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The start edge of the target is brought to the start of the visible area, past the <code>scroll-padding</code> of the
      scroller and the <code>scroll-margin</code> of the target.
    </li>
    <li>
      The visitor's own scrolling stops the scroll: a wheel turn, a touch, a pointer press or a key press. So does a new
      scroll of the same container.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>focus: true</code> gives the focus to the target on arrival, without scrolling again. A target that cannot take
      focus gets <code>tabindex="-1"</code>, which goes back when the focus leaves it.
    </li>
    <li>
      While the scroll runs, the <code>overflow-anchor</code> of the scroller is <code>none</code>, and it is given back at
      the end.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="variants">Variants</h2>
  <h3 id="scroll-the-page-to-a-section">Scroll the page to a section</h3>
  <p>
    Without <code>container</code>, the scroll moves the nearest scrollable ancestor of the target, or the page: the target
    needs no box around it.
  </p>
  <Code data-code={scrollToSection} data-language="ts" />

  <h3 id="stop-a-scroll">Stop a scroll</h3>
  <p>
    The scroll returns <code>&#123; finished, cancel() &#125;</code>. Call <code>cancel()</code> to stop it where it is:
    <code>finished</code> then resolves to <code>'cancelled'</code>. Wait for <code>finished</code> to know when the scroll
    has ended, however it ends.
  </p>

  <h3 id="read-where-a-scroll-stops">Read where a scroll stops</h3>
  <p>
    <code>scrollDestination(target, options)</code>, from <code>@udixio/motion/scroll</code>, returns the
    <code>&#123; x, y &#125;</code> position where the same scroll would stop, without scrolling. Use it to know whether a
    scroll is needed at all.
  </p>

  <h2 id="options">Options</h2>
  <p>
    <code>scrollTo(target, options)</code> takes these options. The target is an element, or a
    <code>&#123; x, y &#125;</code> position.
  </p>
  <MotionOptions
    rows={[
      { name: 'container', values: 'an element, or the window', default: 'the nearest scrollable ancestor, or the page', description: 'The element that scrolls. By default, the nearest scrollable ancestor of the target scrolls.' },
      { name: 'timing', values: 'spring.* token', default: 'spring.slow.spatial', description: 'The spring of the scroll.' },
      { name: 'offset', values: '{ x?, y? } in pixels', default: 'none', description: 'Pixels added to the destination on each axis, in scroll units: a negative y stops short of the target.' },
      { name: 'focus', values: 'true · false', default: 'false', description: 'Gives the focus to the target on arrival, without scrolling again.' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2 id="good-to-know">Good to know</h2>
  <GoodToKnow>
    <Fact label="Reduced motion">
      <p>
        Under the reduced-motion <a href={glossaryHref('policy')}>policy</a>, or with <code>--udx-motion: none</code> on the
        element, the scroll jumps to its destination, and the focus still goes to the target. See
        <a href="/motion/accessibility">Accessibility</a>.
      </p>
    </Fact>
    <Fact label="Timing">
      <p>
        The scroll moves on the slow spatial spring unless <code>timing</code> names another <a href={glossaryHref('token')}>token</a>.
      </p>
      <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.slow.spatial']} />
    </Fact>
    <Fact label="Limits">
      <ul>
        <ForFramework as="li">
          <Fragment slot="html">If the script runs before the queue is in the page, <code>getElementById()</code> returns <code>null</code>: load the script after the markup.</Fragment>
          <Fragment slot="react">If you read <code>ref.current</code> during render, it is <code>null</code>: call <code>scrollTo()</code> from a handler or an effect.</Fragment>
          <Fragment slot="angular">If you read a <code>viewChild</code> before the view exists, Angular throws: read it after <code>ngAfterViewInit</code>.</Fragment>
          <Fragment slot="svelte">If you call <code>scrollTo()</code> before the component mounts, the <code>bind:this</code> variable is still <code>undefined</code>: call it from a handler.</Fragment>
        </ForFramework>
        <li>If a fixed header covers the target, set <code>scroll-padding-top</code> on the scroller, or pass a negative <code>offset.y</code>: the scroll then stops short of the target by that much.</li>
        <li>If the visitor's own scrolling stopped the scroll before the target was reached, call <code>scrollTo</code> again.</li>
        <li>Something else? See <a href="/motion/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a> and <a href="/motion/limits">Limits</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fact>
  </GoodToKnow>
</MotionPage>
